WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.996 --> 00:00:03.120
(ethereal music)

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.410
- Welcome to Apple's walled garden,

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:08.890
where the company's hardware and software

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:12.730
and services have formed these
very high walls around you

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:15.263
so you can never leave.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.460
If you own an iPhone and at
least one other Apple gadget,

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:26.000
you live here with me, in this
metaphorical walled garden,

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.760
which for journalistic purposes brought us

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.440
to this real walled garden.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.350
In tech, a walled garden is a
closed ecosystem of products

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:34.890
and with antitrust scrutiny

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.760
and the Epic versus Apple lawsuit,

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.177
lately you hear a lot about Apple's-

00:00:39.177 --> 00:00:40.300
- [Reporter] Walled garden.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.250
Walled garden.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.230
Walled garden!

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.660
- [Jennifer] To lock in
users to its walled garden.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.240
So how did Apple's walls get so high?

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.390
And is it even a bad thing

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.040
that it's difficult to climb out

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.930
and explore products from other companies

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:55.313
like Google, Samsung, and Microsoft?

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.123
When did these walls get so high?

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.020
Come take a garden tour with me.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.040
I'm gonna help you understand.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.740
This real walled garden,
here in Yonkers, New York,

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:09.250
has many different
areas, just like Apple's.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.860
Let's start here at the entrance
with your Apple hardware.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.440
Without it, you can't enter the garden.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.600
No, these sections are not to scale,

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.280
definitely not according to Apple revenue.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.300
Your journey into the garden
likely started with an iPhone.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.350
Then you bought an Apple Watch or AirPods

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.730
because they worked so
well with the iPhone.

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:31.210
And maybe you also have
an iPad or MacBook.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.530
And now you're here forever.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.390
Many companies like Samsung and Google

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:37.200
have families of products too,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.890
but what makes Apple
stronger is its full control

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:41.830
of the next part of the garden.

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:44.460
This big section here is Apple's software.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:49.350
That means iOS, MacOS,
and WatchOS, and iPadOS.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.740
All of these have Apple-only features

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.510
that tie them together
to work better together.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:57.890
They don't work with Android
phones or Windows computers.

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:00.390
The list is long, but let's look at a few.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:01.560
There's iCloud.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.630
This syncs all your photos, documents,

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.300
notes, et cetera across devices.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.460
There's AirDrop.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.020
This makes it easy to share a file

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.970
from one Apple device to another.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:11.840
There's AirPlay.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.900
This makes it easier
to play music or video,

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.980
say Apple TV+'s own "Ted Lasso",

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.570
from an Apple device on
another Apple device.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:21.760
There's the underlying continuity.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.050
This lets you hand off a web page, email,

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.590
or another activity quickly

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.390
from your iPhone on another Apple device.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:32.170
And then there's the
ultimate one, iMessage.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:35.680
Apple's messaging system lets
you send and receive messages

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:40.580
and stickers and games and
money, but only on Apple devices

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.130
and to people with Apple devices.

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:45.760
It's the ultimate blue bubble wall.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.370
The final big area of the
garden, Apple services.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:50.910
In this first services area,

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.430
you have everything from Apple TV+,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:57.210
what's a computer, to Apple
Music to Apple Fitness+

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.400
Pushups, here they are, everyone.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.210
With most of these, you have to pay more

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:03.293
in the form of monthly subscriptions.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:06.950
Good thing I'm in this garden by myself.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:09.740
And Apple often uses its
power over the platform

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:10.750
to persuade you.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:14.100
For instance, this perpetual
pop-up in my settings menu.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.530
Some of these services are available

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:18.250
for other gardens, like
Android or Windows,

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:21.060
but the experience is always
better on an Apple device.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.950
And then there's one very big
service here, the App Store.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:29.160
Think of it as a large pool
inside a walled garden.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.610
The iOS App Store is the
only app store allowed

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:33.230
on iPhones and iPads.

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:36.720
There are lots of apps in the
pool, but Apple's the fish,

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.540
eating 15% to 30% of paid app revenue

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:42.700
and even digital in-app purchases.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:46.070
Oh, that one's eating 30% of
that app revenue right there.

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:47.500
This has been at the heart of complaints

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.080
from other tech companies,
like in the Epic Games lawsuit.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:52.470
Zoom out to see the whole map

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:54.670
and it's quite the business lesson.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:56.470
Every part keeps you with Apple

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:58.630
and spending more money with Apple.

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:00.450
The first third is what gets you in.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.730
The second, where it
gets you to start using

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.310
and buying even more from section one.

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.530
The third, you buy more stuff,

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.450
except it's digital stuff on your devices.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:11.560
Study that if you want to build

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:13.960
the next $2 trillion company.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.170
So you can see as the garden grows,

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.490
the walls grow higher and higher,

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:20.670
so it's even harder to get out of here.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:22.290
But is it really all that bad?

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:23.430
It's called a walled garden

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:27.020
because the stuff inside is
very pretty and works great,

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:28.950
so you may not even want
a peek over the wall

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:31.570
to see what Samsung or
Microsoft or Google has

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:32.800
on the other side.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.610
And Apple argues that many of its walls

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:37.630
protect user privacy and security.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:39.900
Specifically, it says
it's walled App Store

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.890
keeps out malware and other bad actors.

00:04:42.890 --> 00:04:44.580
The not-so-great stuff starts to show

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.895
when you try to bring a
competitor into the garden,

00:04:46.895 --> 00:04:48.105
(ominous distorted wind whooshing)

00:04:48.105 --> 00:04:48.938
(whispers) Android.

00:04:48.938 --> 00:04:52.610
say switching to a Windows
laptop or an Android phone.

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:55.570
The serenity of the
garden can be disrupted.

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:58.130
Since there's no iMessage
for Android, for instance,

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:01.410
you can suddenly miss out
on contacts or even groups.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.280
In fact, in an internal email surfaced

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.527
in the Epic versus Apple
trial, an Apple executive said,

00:05:06.527 --> 00:05:08.407
"iMessage on Android would simply serve

00:05:08.407 --> 00:05:09.757
"to remove an obstacle

00:05:09.757 --> 00:05:13.620
"to iPhone families giving
their kids Android phones."

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.890
Hey, if Apple's garden is so good,

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:18.650
why does it need these
walls to keep us in?

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:21.300
There's also the argument
that it stifles innovation.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.740
If Apple keeps making its
own versions of products,

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.800
do we miss out on new
ideas and innovations?

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:28.530
And when it comes to the
App Store swimming pool,

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:31.420
developers argue Apple is
unfairly abusing its power

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:33.720
to charge developers more.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:35.950
While the legal battles about
this garden will play out

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:39.200
in anti-trust suits and more
over the next few years,

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.030
there are a few ways Apple
users can poke little holes

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:43.820
in Apple's garden walls.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:46.100
If there's a good alternative
to an Apple service,

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:49.150
like Spotify for Apple
Music, give it a real chance.

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:50.810
You shouldn't just choose to use services

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.380
because they're right
there on our devices.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.800
Then if you pay for a subscription
through the App Store,

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.210
find out what the app
charges on its own website

00:05:58.210 --> 00:05:59.880
for the same service.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.650
You could get a surprise discount.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:03.460
Also, Apple has over the years allowed you

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.130
to change your default
web browser or email app.

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:08.300
So, no, you might not
even want to climb out

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.590
or dig out, but the breaking
a few holes in the walls

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:14.120
might let a little more sunshine in.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:17.560
Hey Tim, just iMessage
on Android and Windows.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:18.720
Think about it.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:19.720
Just think about it.

